= La isla

The channel opens the way these things do — a name passed through three cities, a meeting in a fourth — and the offer is the one Havana has quietly made to every serious armed movement in the Americas: send your best, and they come back trained. Not pamphlet-trained. Trained in the things the sierra teaches too slowly and too expensively: explosives that work, medicine that saves the wounded you are currently losing, the discipline of a column that has been drilled instead of improvised, the tradecraft that keeps a network alive. Months on the island, and a cadre returns worth ten of what he was.

The price is not money. It is entanglement, and exposure, and the long shadow of a patron: the DFS photographs everyone who flies to Havana, the Party's Moscow-liners will call it adventurism and the independents will call it a leash, and somewhere a file thickens with the word *Cuba* stamped across your movement's name. Every guerrilla that took the offer got stronger. Some of them also got a master.

- @isla_enviar: Send them. The war is being lost partly on skills you cannot teach in the sierra; go get them, and manage the shadow.
- @isla_selectivo: Send a handful, quietly — the medics and the explosives people only, the skills that save lives, and keep the political entanglement to a minimum.
- @isla_rechazar: Decline. The Mexican revolution will be made by Mexicans with Mexican hands, or it is not worth making; no patrons, no islands, no leashes.

@isla_enviar
go-to: post_event
on-arrival: internac_armada = 2; inteligencia += 6; parque += 3; disciplina += 5; moral_columnas += 3; expediente += 4; ax_sovietico += 3; sup_middle -= 2

They come back in ones and twos over a year and the difference is immediate and undeniable: the wounded who used to die now sometimes live, the operations that used to be improvised are now planned, the column that used to be a brave crowd is now something closer to an army. It is the biggest single upgrade the war will ever receive. It also arrives with the shadow attached — the file with *Cuba* on it, the middle class that will believe every word the regime now says about foreign hands, the faint permanent question of whose war this is becoming. You decided the skills were worth the shadow. On the day a trained medic keeps a compañero alive, you will not doubt it. On the day the regime's propaganda lands because it is, this once, half true, you will.

@isla_selectivo
go-to: post_event
on-arrival: internac_armada = 2; inteligencia += 3; disciplina += 2; moral_columnas += 2; expediente += 2

You take the scalpel, not the whole hand: two medics, a demolitions cadre, the skills that keep your people breathing, and nothing that requires the movement to sign anything. It is the prudent version and it delivers a prudent fraction of the benefit — fewer wounded lost, a few operations that finally work — while keeping the *Cuba* file thin enough to deny. The purists are half-satisfied and the maximalists grumble that you flew all that way for a first-aid course. But the compañeros who would have died and now do not are not an abstraction, and they were the whole reason you went.

@isla_rechazar
go-to: post_event
on-arrival: ax_sovietico -= 3; disciplina -= 1; prestigio_socialismo += 2; leyenda += 1

You keep the war Mexican down to its wounds: no island, no patron, no file with a foreign stamp, the skills learned the hard way in the sierra at the price the sierra charges, which is paid in the people who die of the things a trained medic would have saved. It is a real principle and it has real graves. The movement that results is unmistakably its own — no one will ever credibly call it Havana's franchise — and it is measurably weaker for the refusal, slower to learn the things that keep guerrillas alive. Independence, here as everywhere in this game, is not free. It is only the one price you can be certain no one else is setting.

@la_jcr
