= La coordinadora continental

The refugees bring it with them — the survivors of coups that ran south to north like a fuse, Chileans after the stadium, Argentines and Uruguayans and Bolivians fleeing the thing that will later have a name, Operation Condor, the continent's dictatorships pooling their torturers across borders. Some of these exiles are not merely victims; they are cadres, veterans of the Tupamaros and the ERP and the MIR, and they carry a proposal that is either the future or a mirror: a *Junta Coordinadora Revolucionaria*, the armed left of the whole hemisphere coordinated as one, since the enemy has already coordinated as one.

It is seductive and it is dangerous in exactly the same proportion. Coordination means arms channels, shared intelligence, a war that is bigger than your sierra. It also means importing doctrines forged in Montevideo's streets into Guerrero's mountains, and painting your movement, in the regime's ready brush, as one more foreign tentacle — and inviting Condor's gaze, which does not respect the border you are counting on.

- @jcr_sumarse: Join the coordination. The dictatorships pool their Condor; the revolution must pool its answer, or be hunted one country at a time.
- @jcr_solidaridad: Shelter the exiles, learn what they know, but stay out of the formal structure — solidarity without a shared command, and without Condor's file.
- @jcr_nacional: Keep the war strictly national. Grieve the continent's dead, house its refugees, and refuse to make Guerrero a front in a hemispheric war it cannot win.

@jcr_sumarse
go-to: post_event
choose-if: disciplina >= 45
unavailable-subtitle: Joining a continental command takes a movement disciplined enough to survive the doctrines and dangers it imports — yours is too loose to absorb them safely.
on-arrival: jcr = 2; parque += 3; inteligencia += 4; linea_guerra += 5; expediente += 5; represion += 3; prestigio_socialismo += 3; leyenda += 1

You sign into the continental war, and it delivers what it promised in both directions: arms move through channels that did not exist before, the intelligence on the shared enemy sharpens, your isolated sierra becomes a node in something vast enough to feel, for the first time, like history instead of a last stand. And it delivers the shadow too — the imported urban doctrines that fit Montevideo better than the Montaña, the regime's propaganda handed a gift it could not have manufactured, and the cold attention of Condor, which files your movement alongside the ones already being disappeared across three borders. You are part of the hemisphere's revolution now. You are also, now, part of what it is losing, in most of the places it is being fought.

@jcr_solidaridad
go-to: post_event
on-arrival: inteligencia += 3; sup_students += 2; prestigio_socialismo += 2; exiliados += 1; expediente += 2

You take the comrades and leave the command: the exiles sheltered, their hard-won knowledge absorbed, the safe houses of two continents quietly linked by trust rather than by a signed structure the enemy can map. It is the choice that keeps the solidarity real and the exposure limited — no formal tentacle for the regime to point at, no line item in Condor's ledger with your name beside it, but also no shared arsenal, no hemispheric weight behind your sierra. You have the friendship and not the front. In a war being lost across a continent, keeping your own war legible and your own the only one you are fighting may be the difference between a defeat and a catastrophe.

@jcr_nacional
go-to: post_event
on-arrival: ax_sovietico -= 2; exiliados += 1; disciplina += 1; prestigio_socialismo += 1

You house the refugees and honor their dead and refuse, absolutely, to make the Montaña a front in a war designed on other continents for other terrain. It is the sober call — Guerrero cannot win Montevideo's war and would only import its defeats — and it costs you the arms and the intelligence and the vast electric feeling of being part of the hemisphere's uprising. The exiles understand and do not fully forgive; internationalism, to people whose countries were taken, is not an optional entanglement but the only hope they have left, and a movement that keeps its war national is, to them, a movement keeping its lifeboat to itself. You have chosen the war you can actually fight. History will record that it was, at least, coherent.
